        <div class="col-md-9" role="main">

          <div class="bs-docs-section">
            <div class="page-header">
              <h1 id="start">MCLCMP: The Multiple Container Loading Cost Minimization Problem</h1>
            </div>
            <p>This page is maintained by <a href="http://www.zhuwb.com">Dr. Wenbin Zhu</a> to suppliment the article  <a href="http://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0377221711003614">The Multiple Container Loading Cost Minimization Problem</a> published in European Journal of Operational Research.</p>

            <p><a href="http://pan.baidu.com/s/1hqGJ436" class="btn btn-primary">Download</a> Data sets, binary executable of our algorithm and detailed soluiton files. When prompted, enter password: <b>ewbq</b></p>

            
            <p><a href="reference.bib" class="btn btn-primary">Bibtex</a> for citing our work.</p>

          </div>


 
          <div class="bs-docs-section">
            <div class="page-header">
              <h1 id="intro">Introduction</h1>
            </div>
            <P>The MCLCMP is defined as follows. We are given a number of rectangular containers
            of <i>M</i> types, represented by <i>C<sub>1</sub>, C<sub>2</sub>, ..., C<sub>M</sub></i>,
            that differ in terms of dimensions and cost. The cost for each type is given by
            <i>c<sub>1</sub>, c<sub>2</sub>, ..., c<sub>M</sub></i>, and there are <i>m<sub>t</sub>,
            1 &le; t &le; M</i> available containers of type <i>t</i>. We are also given a
            number of rectangular boxes of <i>N</i> types with different dimensions, represented
            by <i>B<sub>1</sub>, B<sub>2</sub>, ..., B<sub>N</sub></i>; there are <i>n<sub>j</sub>, 1 &le; j &le; N</i>
            available boxes of type <i>j</i>. The objective of the MCLCMP is to produce a
            set of packing plans such that all boxes are orthogonally packed into the
            containers and the total cost of containers used is minimized. We focus on the
            special case where there are an unlimited number of containers of each type,
            although our technique can be extended to handle a limited number of containers.
            We also do not consider supporting area constraints, although our approach can
            be modified to do so.
            </P>
          </div>

   
          <div class="bs-docs-section">
            <div class="page-header">
              <h1 id="data">Data Sets</h1>
            </div>
            <p>
            We conducted our experiments on two sets of instances. The first set consists of 47
            instances generated by <a href="#Ivancic1989Integer">Ivancic et al. (1989)</a> for the 3D bin packing 
            problem, which can be seen as a special case of MCLCMP with only one type of containers. This set
            of instances are stored in IMM/IMM.txt with the following input format:</p>
            <div class="highlight"><pre><code>
  47               # total number of instances
  1                # ID of instance
  10 6 16          # L, W, H of the container
  2                # the number of box types
  1 2 1 6 1 8 1 20   # id l lv w wv h hv count,
                    #   id: box type id, starting from 1
                    #   l, w, h: length, width and height of a box
                    #   lv,wv,hv: either 1 or 0 indicating whether the box can
                    #      be placed with its length, width and height aligned
                    #      with the vertical direction (H) of the container
                    #   count: available boxes for this type
  2 8 1 4 1 10 1 50  # information about 2nd type of boxes
  2                # information about 2nd instances
  14 14 14
  ....
            </code></pre></div>

            We generated a total of 350 instances with known optimal solutions. They are divided into
            seven groups by the number of box types <i>N</i> with <i>N</i> ranges from three to nine.
            Each group consists of 50 instances. Instances in group <i>N</i> are stored in the file
            MTC/mtc<i>N</i>.txt. Instances are divided into three types depends on the number of container
            types in the known optimal solutions. The first 15 instances in each group is called type 1 instances 
            whose known optimal solutions utilize only the 40-foot high cube containers; the next 15 instances in
            each group is called type 2 instances whose known optimal solutions utilize both 40-foot and 40-foot
            high cube containers; and the last 20 instances in each group is called type 3 instances whose known
            optimal solutions utilize 20-foot, 40-foot and 40-foot high cube containers. The input format for
            mtc<i>N</i>.txt is as follows:
            </p>
            <div class="highlight"><pre><code>
 47               
 1                # ID of instance
 10 6 16          # L, W, H of the container
 2                # the number of box types
 1 2 1 6 1 8 1 20   # id l lv w wv h hv count,
                    #   id: box type id, starting from 1
                    #   l, w, h: length, width and height of a box
                    #   lv,wv,hv: either 1 or 0 indicating whether the box can
                    #      be placed with its length, width and height aligned
                    #      with the vertical direction (H) of the container
                    #   count: available boxes for this type
 2 8 1 4 1 10 1 50  # information about 2nd type of boxes
 2                # information about 2nd instances
 14 14 14
 ....
  50                    # total number of instances
  1,9000.0              # instance ID, cost-of-known-optimal-solution
  3                     # the number of container types
  590,235,239,0,900.0   # L,W,H,count,cost: info for 1st type of container
                        #   L,W,H: the dimension of a container
                        #   count: the number of containers in the known optimal solution
                        #   cost:  the cost per container
  1203,235,239,0,1700.0 # Information about 2nd type of container
  1203,235,270,5,1800.0
  3                     # the number of box types
  1,37,1,135,1,49,1,710 # id, l, lv, w, wv, h, hv, count: info about 1st box type
                        #   l, w, h: the dimension of a box
                        #   lv,wv,hv: either 1 or 0 indicating whether or not the
                        #      length, width and height of the box can be aligned
                        #      with the vertical direction (H) of containers
                        #   count: the available boxes of this type
  2,47,1,115,1,64,1,290 # info about 2nd box type
  3,60,1,69,1,63,1,400
  2,12600.0             # info about 2nd instance
  ...
            </code></pre></div>


          </div>
 



          <div class="bs-docs-section">
            <div class="page-header">
              <h1 id="exec">Binary Executable</h1>
            </div>

            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-danger">
              <h4>Ilog CPLEX 11.0 or later required</h4>
              <p>We assume Ilog CPLEX 12.6 is installed in D:\Program Files\IBM\ILOG\CPLEX_Studio126, you may
                change the command in this section accordingly if your cplex is installed in different
                location</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-danger">
              <h4>Java 1.6.0 or later is required</h4>
            </div>

            <h2>Steps to run our executable</h2>
            <ol>
              <li> Unpack solver-and-solutions.7z using 7zip</li>
              <li> Copy D:\Program Files\IBM\ILOG\CPLEX_Studio126\cplex\lib\cplex.jar to overwrite solver-and-solutions\bin\mclp_v1_lib\cplex.jar</li>
              <li> Open a command prompt and type the following:
            <div class="highlight"><pre><code>
  java -Djava.library.path=<span class="nt">"D:\Program Files\IBM\ILOG\CPLEX_Studio126\cplex\bin\x64_win64"</span>  -jar mclp_v1.jar [input-file] [result-dir] [type] [seed] [R] [alpha]
            </code></pre></div>
              </li>
            </ol>

            <p>There 6 options from left to right:
            <ul>    
              <li>input-file: the location of input file that contains problem instances</li>
              <li>result-dir: the diractory to store resulting files</li>
              <li>type: either imm" or "mtc" indicating the type of instances to be solved</li>
              <li>seed: the random seed used in this excution</li>
              <li>R: the value of the parameter R for this excution</li>
              <li>alpha: the value of parameter <i>a<sub>l</sub></i> in Algorithm 1 page 505</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Here is an example:</p>
            <div class="highlight"><pre><code>
  java -Djava.library.path=<span class="nt">"D:\Program Files\IBM\ILOG\CPLEX_Studio126\cplex\bin\x64_win64"</span> -jar mclp_v1.jar ..\MTC\mtc3.txt mtc3-result mtc 5 5 0.01
            </code></pre></div>


          </div>



          <div class="bs-docs-section">
            <div class="page-header">
              <h1 id="result">Detailed Results</h1>
            </div>

            <p>The result reported in our paper are obtained with the following settings.
            Our SCBS was implemented as a sequential algorithm in Java,
            and no multi-threading was explicitly utilized. It was executed
            on a HP server equipped with one Intel Xeon E5430 CPU clocked
            at 2.66 GHz, 8 GB RAM and running CentOS 5.4. Sun Microsystem’s
            JDK 1.6.0 update 22 and ILog CPlex 11.0 were installed. Both Java
            and CPlex are 32-bit editions. Parameter R and alpha are fixed
            to 5 and 0.01 respectively. Each instance is solved 5 times with seed
            set to 3, 5, 7, 9 and 11 respectively. The detailed soluiton files can be found
            in solver-and-solutions.7z:
            </p>
            <div class="highlight"><pre><code>
IMM/solution-files/seed=3/sol/imm-1.txt     # The soluiton produced by SCBS with seed 3 for first instance in imm
IMM/solution-files/seed=3/sol/imm-2.txt
...
MTC/solution-files/seed=3/sol/mtc3-1.txt    # The soluiton produced by SCBS with seed 3 for first instance in mtc3
...
            </code></pre></div>
          </div>


          <div class="bs-docs-section">
            <div class="page-header">
              <h1 id="err">Errata to our <a href="http://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0377221711003614">published manuscript</a></h1>
            </div>
            <ol>
              <li>Page 503, right column, equation (8): "N<sub>l</sub>", "N<sub>w</sub>"
                and "N<sub>h</sub>" should be replaced by "l", "w" and "h" respectively.
                The correct equation should be:
                <p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; l x<sub>l</sub>&nbsp;I<sub>l</sub>&nbsp;+ w x<sub>w</sub>&nbsp;I<sub>w</sub>&nbsp;+ h x<sub>h</sub>&nbsp;I<sub>h</sub>&nbsp;&le; H</p>
              </li>

              <li><p>Page 511, column 'lb', is computed by&nbsp;Ivancic&nbsp;et al. (1989) with an
                additional constraint that boxes of type 1 can not be placed in the same bin as
                boxes of type 2. Hence it is not valid lower bound for 3D bin packing problem.
                We have recomputed two valid lower bounds:</p>

                <p><b><i>Volume LB</i></b>: the total volume of all boxes divided by volume of bin and rounded up</p>

                <p><b style="font-style:italic">1DBPP</b>: treat each box and bin as 1d object solve the corresponding 1DBPP optimally we get a valid lower bound for 3D BPP</p>

                <p>For 39 out of the 47 IMM instances, Knapsack Volume LB matches the lower bound by&nbsp;Ivancic et al. (1989). For instance number 9,10,11,13,14,35 and 36, Knapsack Volume LB is weaker; for instance 12, Knapsack Volume LB is stronger. The details can be found in <a href="IMM47-LB.xlsx">IMM47-LB.xlsx</a></p>
              </li>
              
              <li> Spotted on 28 Nov 2011. Page 504, Section 4.3.
                <ul>
                  <li>Step 4, number of boxes of type <i>B<sub>1</sub></i> should be 2 instead of 1</li>
                  <li>Step 5, only up to 3 boxes of <i>B<sub>3</sub></i> will be loaded not 4</li>
                  <li>Step 6, <i>n<sub>3</sub></i> = 22 not 21</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
  

